 "result": "{\"answer\": \"Four. The five conventionally named Great Lakes are Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, and Ontario. Because Lake Michigan and Lake Huron are joined at the Straits of Mackinac \u2014 a wide (about 5 miles/8 km), deep channel with no appreciable drop in elevation \u2014 they share the same surface level (about 577 ft / 176 m) and behave hydrologically as a single lake, often called 'Lake Michigan\u2013Huron.' Treating that combined basin as one lake leaves four: Superior, Michigan\u2013Huron, Erie, and Ontario. Incidentally, Lake Michigan\u2013Huron so defined is the largest freshwater lake in the world by surface area (roughly 45,000 sq mi / 117,000 km\u00b2), exceeding Lake Superior.\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"abstained\": false}",
